  data: {
    LLM: '1. Tableau Building: Build down in alternating colors (e.g., a Black 10 on a Red Jack).\n2. Group Moves: Any face-up card, along with all cards sitting on top of it, can be moved as a unit. The cards above the base card do NOT need to be in any specific order.\n3. Foundations: Build up in suit (A-2-3...K). Once a card is on a foundation, it usually cannot be moved back to the tableau.',
    Player: '1. Tableau Building: Build down in alternating colors (e.g., a Black 10 on a Red Jack).\n2. Group Moves: Any face-up card, along with all cards sitting on top of it, can be moved as a unit. The cards above the base card do NOT need to be in any specific order.\n3. Foundations: Build up in suit (A-2-3...K). Once a card is on a foundation, it usually cannot be moved back to the tableau.',
    objective: 'Move all 104 cards to the 8 foundation piles, building each suit up from Ace to King.',
    gameplay: '1. Tableau Building: Build down in alternating colors (e.g., a Black 10 on a Red Jack).\n2. Group Moves: Any face-up card, along with all cards sitting on top of it, can be moved as a unit. The cards above the base card do NOT need to be in any specific order.\n3. Foundations: Build up in suit (A-2-3...K). Once a card is on a foundation, it usually cannot be moved back to the tableau.',
    keyRules: [
      'Only a King (or a group headed by a King) can be moved into an empty tableau space.',
      'Flipping Cards: When a face-down card is exposed by moving the cards above it, flip it over immediately.',
      'With 104 cards, you have 8 Aces and 8 Kings to manage.',
    ],
    bonusRuleGuids: [],
    useTrump: false,
    trumpBonusValues: null,
    setup: {
      players: 'One player only. Double Yukon is a solitaire (patience) game; the player plays alone against the layout, moving cards between tableau columns and to foundations with no opponent or partner.',
      deck: '104 cards (Two identical 52ndcard decks).',
      equipment: 'Two card decks, large playing surface.',
      dealing: 'Cards are dealt into 10 columns. Column 1 has 5 cards (all face-up). Columns 2 through 10 have 7 to 15 cards respectively, with the last 6 cards in each column always face-up and the underlying cards face-down. There is no stock; all cards are on the table.',
    },
    turnFlow: '1. Tableau Building: Build down in alternating colors (e.g., a Black 10 on a Red Jack).\n2. Group Moves: Any face-up card, along with all cards sitting on top of it, can be moved as a unit. The cards above the base card do NOT need to be in any specific order.\n3. Foundations: Build up in suit (A-2-3...K). Once a card is on a foundation, it usually cannot be moved back to the tableau.',
